2026-06-30 15:31:53,303 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-6a37fe85-ffeb-4268-95b6-d56024c75eb9', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep1_s1_66p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep1_s2_49c50p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep1_s3_34c40p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep1_s4_60c10p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep2_s10_86c80p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep2_s11_77c10p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep2_s12_18c80p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep2_s9_58c20p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep3_s17_38c80p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep3_s18_45c40p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep3_s19_41c60p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_IRF1_rep3_s20_22c80p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep1_s5_67c90p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep1_s6_45p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep1_s7_64c80p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep1_s8_21p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep2_s13_62c40p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep2_s14_42p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep2_s15_26c60p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep2_s16_17c30p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_noBiotin_control_s25_33p.raw\n20221014_E3_RSLC1_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_noBiotin_control_s25_52c60p.raw\n20221020_E3_RSLC6_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep3_s21_50c30p.raw\n20221020_E3_RSLC6_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep3_s22_44p.raw\n20221020_E3_RSLC6_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep3_s23_26c90p.raw\n20221020_E3_RSLC6_Fischer_Decker_MPL_UniWien_STAT1_rep3_s24_37c30p.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nIdentifying interactors of IRF1 and STAT1 during type I and type II interferon treatment using proximity-dependent labeling\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIdentifying interactors of IRF1 and STAT1 in bone marrow-derived macrophages during type I and type II interferon treatment using the proximity-dependent labeling approach TurboID followed by Mass Spectrometry\n\n[METHODS]\nCells were seeded in 15cm dishes 2 days before harvesting. Single cell clone of RAW264.7 Stat1-/- cells transduced with STAT1-TurboID-V5 as well as V5-TurboID-NLS were treated for 24 hours with Doxycycline (final concentration: 2μg/ml). Single cell clone of RAW264.7 Irf1-/- cells transduced with V5-TurboID-IRF1 as well as V5-TurboID-NLS were treated with 2μg/ml Doxycycline for 4 hours. Cells were either treated with IFNβ or IFNγ for 3 hours and biotinylation was induced by adding 500μM Biotin for 10 minutes. Single cell clone of RAW264.7 Stat1-/- cells transduced with V5-TurboID-STAT1 as well as V5-TurboID-NLS were treated with 2μg/ml Doxycycline for 24 hours. Cells were either treated with IFNβ or IFNγ for 3 hours and biotinylation was induced by adding 500μM Biotin for 10 minutes. The experiment was performed in biological triplicates. Cells were washed 3 times in ice cold PBS, cell number counted, and aliquots (2x106 cells per tube) snap frozen in liquid nitrogen before storing them at -80°C. 2x106 cells were lysed for 15 minutes on ice in CEB buffer (10mM Hepes, 10mM KCl, 1.5mM MgCl2, 0.1% NP-40 and complete EDTA-free protease inhibitor cocktail). Cells were centrifuged for 5 minutes at 500rcf and 4°C. Nuclear pellet was washed 3 times with wash buffer A (10mM Hepes, 10mM KCl, 1.5mM MgCl2, 0.1% NP-40 and EDTA-free protease inhibitor cocktail). Pellet was lysed in 200μl NEB (25mM Hepes pH 7.9, 1.5M MgCl2, 0.1M EDTA) supplemented with EDTA-free protease inhibitor cocktail, 1mM PMSF, 1mM Na2VO3 and 550mM NaCl. Nuclei were vortexed 5 times every 10 minutes for 10 seconds while being kept on ice, centrifuged for 5 minutes at 16.000rcf at 4°C and transferred to a fresh, protein low bind tube. Protein concentration was measured using Pierce™ BCA Protein Assay kit (Thermo-Fisher Scientific). 200μg protein was added to 100μl acetylated beads 75 and filled up with NEB to reach a final concentration of 200mM NaCl. Samples were incubated overnight at 4°C while rotating. Next day, beads were washed once for 5 minutes with NEB, three times for 5 minutes with RIPA buffer (0.1% SDS, 0.5% Sodium Deoxycholate, 1% NP40 substitute, 50mM Tris, 150mM NaCl, 1mM Na3VO4, 2mM NaF, 2mM PMSF, Protease Inhibitor) and 6 times with TBS (50mM Tris, 150mM NaCl pH 7.5).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nMus musculus (mouse)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
